      "description": "Bottom letterbox bar during the film; cuts in on one frame.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-film\"><span class=\"ds-film-step\">41</span><span class=\"ds-film-date\">Wednesday 23 September</span><button class=\"ds-film-stop\"><svg width=\"12\" height=\"12\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"3\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\"/></svg>Stop</button></div>",
      "css": ".ds-film{--paper:#dcd8cf;--panel:#f1eee7;--ink:#232420;--ink-2:#57564f;--hair:rgba(35,36,32,.16);--signal:#f04a00;--wash:rgba(35,36,32,.07);font-family:'Google Sans Flex Variable','Helvetica Neue',sans-serif;font-variation-settings:'ROND' 100;height:72px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;padding:0 32px;background:#0c0c0b;color:#efece5}.ds-film-step{font-size:40px;font-weight:730;letter-spacing:-.04em;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.ds-film-date{font-size:14px;font-weight:560}.ds-film-stop{margin-left:auto;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;height:32px;padding:0 12px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid rgba(239,236,229,.3);background:transparent;color:#efece5;font:inherit;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer}.ds-film-stop:focus-visible{outline:2px solid #f04a00;outline-offset:2px}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Brickfilm Set",
    "overview": "Brickwork is a miniature stop-motion set on a desk. Glossy toy bricks stand in towers on a dark studded baseplate, over a paper sweep that curves up into a back wall. The set follows the room's light: the Mac's appearance picks the mood (`prefers-color-scheme`, or `?day` and `?night` to force it). Day is a neutral studio under a soft white key. Night is the same sweep in a dark studio under one warm lamp whose pool falls off into the dark.\n\nThe interface is printed ink sitting on that set, never a panel system laid over it. Two ink weights, one signal orange, and a drawn isometric brick do all the work. Colour belongs to the bricks. Motion runs at 12 frames per second in whole frames, so a jump in time plays as stop motion and the film cuts in and out on a single frame.\n\nDensity is low. The first viewport is the set, full bleed, with the step numeral top left, tools top right, and the goal pads bottom centre with the day strip just above them.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "A full-bleed WebGL set with two moods (day, night) that share one geometry.",
      "Bricks are physical: clearcoat ABS, rounded edges, studs, a seam between neighbours.",
      "UI is two inks plus one signal orange; colour lives in the bricks.",
      "Everything that animates steps at 12 fps (83.3 ms per frame).",
      "Google Sans Flex with the rounded axis at 100, heavy tabular numerals.",
      "One stroke icon family (Lucide), drawn bricks for every goal reference."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Colour Lives In Bricks Rule",
        "body": "Saturated colour appears only on bricks and brick glyphs. The HTML layer is ink, paper and one orange.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never Tinted Rule",
        "body": "A goal's colour is never tinted, dimmed or recoloured for state. Selection, hover and today are shown with marks, weight and washes of ink, not by changing a brick.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Gold Rule",
        "body": "Gold is reserved for the 100th brick milestone. Nothing else is gold.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Signal Is Scarce Rule",
        "body": "Orange marks the film transport, the selected day and keyboard focus. It never fills a second button.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Step Numeral Leads Rule",
        "body": "The biggest type on screen is always a day count, set heavy and tabular, like a build booklet step. No sentence is ever set larger than it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Count Rule",
        "body": "Every number that changes (step, part counts, pad counts, day numbers) uses `tabular-nums` so it can tick frame by frame without the line moving.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lit Not Lifted Rule",
        "body": "Nothing in the HTML layer floats on a shadow. If something needs to read as raised, it is a brick, and the set's light does it.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do step every animation on the 12 fps frame (83.3 ms) and snap to whole poses.",
      "Do cut the film in and out on one frame.",
      "Do draw goals as BrickGlyphs in the goal colour, at the glyph scales above.",
      "Do keep spacing on half studs (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32px).",
      "Do swap only the ink, paper, panel and hairline tokens between day and night; the set swaps through `LOOKS`.",
      "Do set every changing number in tabular figures.",
      "Do use Lucide icons at one stroke family (1.75 for 18px tools, 2 to 2.5 for small transport icons)."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't tint, dim or recolour a goal's bricks for state.",
      "Don't use gold for anything but the 100th brick.",
      "Don't fill a second button with Signal Orange.",
      "Don't put box shadows on the HTML layer.",
      "Don't fade or ease the film, drops or count ticks.",
      "Don't add streak flames, checklists, a contribution heatmap or bar charts.",
      "Don't move a placed brick; the build only grows."
    ]
  }
}
